--- note
## Why a whole section
As open models become "central to enterprise infrastructure," the report argues **provenance and
security are paramount**. Gemma 4 undergoes "the same rigorous safety evaluations as Gemini models,"
and treats responsibility, safety, and security as first-class development concerns.

--- note
## Governance & assessment
The approach weighs the **benefits of openness** (spreading the technology's benefits across society)
against the **risk of malicious use** causing individual and institutional harm (Weidinger et al.,
2021). Models were built **in partnership with internal safety and responsible-AI teams**, with
scrutiny of evolving LLM risks and how models get deployed in the wild.

--- note
## Safety policies (train-time mitigations)
A key pillar is aligning fine-tuned models with Google's AI principles. The policies aim to prevent
generation of:

- **CSAM** and child exploitation content,
- **dangerous** content (e.g. promoting suicide, real-world-harm instructions),
- **sexually explicit** content,
- **hate speech** (dehumanizing protected groups),
- **harassment** (encouraging violence).

Mitigations: careful **input data pre-processing**, **filtering training data to remove personal
information** and other sensitive data (privacy), plus **post-training evaluations and train-time
mitigations** aligned to those policies.

--- note
## Safety evaluations
Automated and human evaluations were run across all safety areas. The report claims **major
improvements in every content-safety category** relative to previous Gemma models, with **minimal
policy violations** for both text-to-text and image-to-text, across all sizes — **while keeping
unjustified refusals low** (a real tension: over-refusal is itself a failure). Importantly, testing was
done **without safety filters**, to measure the model's *inherent* behavior. They frame this within
Google DeepMind's **Frontier Safety Framework** (2024).

--- note
## Ethical considerations
Three areas are called out for developers who build on Gemma 4:

- **Bias & fairness** — text/image training data can carry socio-cultural bias; the report urges
  continuous monitoring and de-biasing during fine-tuning.
- **Misinformation & misuse** — mitigated with technical limitations, developer education, and the
  **Responsible Generative AI Toolkit**.
- **Privacy** — datasets were filtered for personal info, but developers must still follow local
  privacy law and use privacy-preserving techniques.

The stated stance on open release: adopt safety mitigations **proportionate to risk**, and share models
**only when confident the benefits significantly outweigh foreseeable risks**.
